## 📥 Installation

```bash
go get github.com/AnandSGit/hybridpipe.io
```

## 🚀 Quick Start

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"
    
    hp "github.com/AnandSGit/hybridpipe.io"
)

func main() {
    // Register custom types (only needed for gob encoding)
    type Person struct {
        Name string
        Age  int
    }
    hp.Enable(Person{})
    
    // Create a router (e.g., NATS)
    router, err := hp.DeployRouter(hp.NATS)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatalf("Failed to create router: %v", err)
    }
    defer router.Close()
    
    // Subscribe to a pipe
    err = router.Accept("example.pipe", func(data any) {
        if person, ok := data.(Person); ok {
            fmt.Printf("Received person: %s, %d years old\n", person.Name, person.Age)
        }
    })
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatalf("Failed to subscribe: %v", err)
    }
    
    // Send a message
    person := Person{Name: "John Doe", Age: 30}
    err = router.Dispatch("example.pipe", person)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatalf("Failed to send message: %v", err)
    }
    
    // Keep the program running
    select {}
}
```

## 🔌 Supported Protocols

| Protocol | Description | Best For |
|----------|-------------|----------|
| **NATS** | Lightweight pub/sub messaging system | High-throughput, low-latency messaging |
| **Kafka** | Distributed streaming platform | High-throughput, durable messaging |
| **RabbitMQ** | Message broker implementing AMQP | Reliable messaging with complex routing |
| **AMQP 1.0** | Advanced Message Queuing Protocol | Enterprise messaging |
| **MQTT** | Lightweight pub/sub for IoT | IoT and mobile applications |
| **NSQ** | Realtime distributed messaging platform | Simple, distributed messaging |
| **TCP/IP** | Direct TCP/IP communication | Custom protocols, direct communication |
| **Redis** | In-memory data structure store | Simple pub/sub, caching integration |
| **NetChan** | Go channels over network | Go inter-process communication |
| **Qpid** | Apache Qpid implementation | Enterprise integration |
| **Mock** | In-memory implementation | Testing without external dependencies |

## 🏗️ Architecture

HybridPipe.io is built with a modular architecture:

```
hybridpipe.io/
├── core/                  # Core interfaces and functionality
├── protocols/             # Protocol implementations
│   ├── amqp/              # AMQP 1.0 implementation
│   ├── kafka/             # Kafka implementation
│   ├── mqtt/              # MQTT implementation
│   ├── nats/              # NATS implementation
│   ├── netchan/           # NetChan implementation
│   ├── nsq/               # NSQ implementation
│   ├── qpid/              # Qpid implementation
│   ├── rabbitmq/          # RabbitMQ implementation
│   ├── redis/             # Redis implementation
│   ├── tcp/               # TCP implementation
│   └── mock/              # Mock implementation
├── serialization/         # Serialization functionality
├── tracing/               # Tracing functionality
├── monitoring/            # Monitoring functionality
└── middleware/            # Middleware components
```

The core of the system is the `HybridPipe` interface:

```go
type HybridPipe interface {
    // Connect establishes a connection to the messaging system.
    Connect() error

    // Dispatch sends a message to the specified pipe.
    Dispatch(pipe string, data any) error

    // Accept subscribes to messages from the specified pipe and processes them with the provided function.
    Accept(pipe string, fn Process) error

    // Remove unsubscribes from the specified pipe.
    Remove(pipe string) error

    // Close terminates the connection to the messaging system.
    Close()
}
```

## 📝 Examples

### Basic Usage

```go
// Create a router
router, _ := hp.DeployRouter(hp.NATS)
defer router.Close()

// Subscribe to a pipe
router.Accept("example.pipe", func(data any) {
    fmt.Printf("Received: %v\n", data)
})

// Send a message
router.Dispatch("example.pipe", "Hello, HybridPipe!")
```

### Using Different Protocols

```go
// Service A: Publishes events to Kafka for durability
kafkaRouter, _ := hp.DeployRouter(hp.KAFKA)
kafkaRouter.Dispatch("user.created", userData)

// Service B: Uses NATS for real-time notifications
natsRouter, _ := hp.DeployRouter(hp.NATS)
natsRouter.Dispatch("notifications", notification)
```

### IoT Data Collection

```go
// IoT Gateway: Collects data from devices via MQTT
mqttRouter, _ := hp.DeployRouter(hp.MQTT)
mqttRouter.Accept("sensors/+/temperature", func(data any) {
    // Process sensor data
    // Forward to data processing pipeline
    kafkaRouter.Dispatch("sensor-data", data)
})
```

### NetChan Example

```go
// Create a NetChan adapter
adapter, _ := hp.NewNetChanAdapter(10)

// Get send and receive channels
sendCh := adapter.SendChan("example-channel")
recvCh := adapter.RecvChan("example-channel")

// Send using Go channel syntax
sendCh <- "Hello, NetChan!"

// Receive using Go channel syntax
msg := <-recvCh
fmt.Println(msg)
```

## ⚙️ Configuration

HybridPipe.io can be configured using a TOML configuration file:

```toml
[NATS]
NServers = "localhost"
NLPort = 4222

[KAFKA]
KServers = "localhost"
KLPort = 9092

[RABBITMQ]
RServerPort = "amqp://guest:guest@localhost:5672/"

# ... other protocol configurations
```

The configuration file can be placed in:
- Current directory (`hybridpipe_db.toml`)
- `/etc/hybridpipe/hybridpipe_db.toml`
- `/opt/hybridpipe/config/hybridpipe_db.toml`
- `$HOME/.config/hybridpipe/hybridpipe_db.toml`

Or specified using the `HYBRIDPIPE_CONFIG` environment variable.

## 🔧 Advanced Usage

### Serialization Options

```go
// Create serialization options
options := hp.SerializationOptions{
    Format:      hp.FormatJSON,
    Compression: true,
}

// Encode with options
bytes, _ := hp.EncodeWithOptions(data, options)

// Decode with options
var result MyType
hp.DecodeWithOptions(bytes, &result, options)
```

### Tracing and Monitoring

```go
// Create a tracer
tracer, _ := hp.NewTracer(hp.TracerConfig{
    ServiceName: "my-service",
    Exporter: hp.ExporterConfig{
        Type: "jaeger",
    },
})

// Create a meter
meter, _ := hp.NewMeter(hp.MeterConfig{
    ServiceName: "my-service",
})

// Wrap a router with tracing and monitoring
router, _ := hp.DeployRouter(hp.NATS)
tracedRouter := hp.WrapRouterWithMetrics(router, tracer, meter)

// Use the traced router
tracedRouter.Dispatch("example.pipe", data)
```

